From: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 13:40:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: offer a flowtable to the switch first

A netfilter flowtable bound to a DSA user port reaches hardware through
dsa_user_setup_ft_block(), which forwards the block to the conduit netdev.
That route exists for a flow engine that sits on the conduit, as MediaTek's
does. A switch that owns the flow tables itself has no way to be offered
the block other than by taking it back off its own conduit, through
conduit->dsa_ptr.

That indirection does not survive teardown. dsa_tree_teardown() releases
the conduit before the ports, so by the time a user port unregisters and
netfilter issues FLOW_BLOCK_UNBIND, conduit->dsa_ptr is already NULL and
the forward returns -EOPNOTSUPP. netfilter treats the refusal as "nothing
to release" and the block callback stays on the driver's list, holding a
pointer to memory the switch is about to free.

Offer TC_SETUP_FT to the switch first and keep the conduit forward as the
fallback, so a switch-side flow engine is reached through the port that
owns it and a conduit-side one is reached exactly as before. The six
drivers that implement .port_setup_tc all return -EOPNOTSUPP for a type
they do not handle, so none of them changes behaviour.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
---
--- a/net/dsa/user.c
+++ b/net/dsa/user.c
@@ -1743,11 +1743,22 @@ static int dsa_user_setup_tc(struct net_
 {
 	struct dsa_port *dp = dsa_user_to_port(dev);
 	struct dsa_switch *ds = dp->ds;
+	int err;
 
 	switch (type) {
 	case TC_SETUP_BLOCK:
 		return dsa_user_setup_tc_block(dev, type_data);
 	case TC_SETUP_FT:
+		/* A switch that owns the flow tables answers for itself; only
+		 * a conduit-side flow engine needs the block forwarded, and
+		 * that forward is gone by the time the port is torn down.
+		 */
+		if (ds->ops->port_setup_tc) {
+			err = ds->ops->port_setup_tc(ds, dp->index, type,
+						     type_data);
+			if (err != -EOPNOTSUPP)
+				return err;
+		}
 		return dsa_user_setup_ft_block(ds, dp->index, type_data);
 	default:
 		break;
